ially gasoline running dangerously  low Rommel was forced to retreat with  his mobile units leaving most of his  infantry to be captured now as rommel's  turn to go on the run with Montgomery  chasing after him that chase would end  in Tunisia in May 1943 when the eighth  Army now joined up with Allied units who  had invaded Morocco in Operation Torch  forced what was left of the Africa  quarter surrender Rommel Hitler's  favorite General was evacuated  beforehand El alamin was the first major  Allied victory in the European theater  of the war and overnight Monty was  everybody's favorite General he was  given a Knighthood and promoted and the  British press were quick to eat Laurels  upon him proclaiming him the best  British general since the Duke of  Wellington the most important impression  he was to make though was not with the  British public but with his American  Allies and it wasn't a good one  foreign  operation was the invasion of the  Italian island of Sicily operation Husky  for the first time Montgomery in the  eighth Army would be working in concert  with the Americans including his new  commanding officer General Dwight D  Eisenhower Eisenhower had a difficult  job as the commander of all allied  forces in the European theater trying to  balance the expectations of his bosses  in Washington with those are the other  members of the western Allied Coalition  and successfully fight the war at the  same time Monty didn't make things easy  for him when it came to operation husky  however Montgomery insisted that the  plans for the operation were all wrong  and threatened to resign if the plan he  put forward wasn't followed to the  letter just as he'd expected Monty got  his way but he had encountered on the  commander of the U.S 7th Army General  George Patton being just as much of a  premid honor as he was Patton nicknamed  all blood and guts was similar in many  ways to Montgomery both had difficult  relationships with their superiors while  being beloved by their subordinates they  craved glory on the battlefield and the  publicity it brought and they were  skilled Generals in Mobile armored  Warfare in hindsight they were probably  too alike to be expected to work  together without controversy the  invasion of Sicily was ultimately  successful but because the two Army  commanders spent more time trying to  one-up each other than working together  over a hundred thousand axis troops that  might have been cut off and captured  managed to escape to the Italian  Mainland with patterns sidelined by  press reports that he slapped and  berated Two Soldiers suffering from  battle fatigue which is today known as  PTSD Montgomery's next job as eighth  Army Commander was the invasion of the  Italian Peninsula once again the  campaign was marked by poor Allied  coordination in the face of determined  German resistance the Italians having  switched sides and surrendered to the  Allies by this point Montgomery bitterly  complained in his diary and to his  superiors in London about the disjointed  effort but the uncomfortable truth was  that the Battle of Italy was a sideshow  and everyone knew it the real Hammer  blow would come in France where the  Allies were preparing The cross-channel  Invasion known as operation Overlord  the plan was to land American British  and Canadian troops on the beaches of  Normandy punching through the  fortifications of Hitler's Fortress  Europe liberating Paris from the Nazis  grip and pushing on to Germany itself  once again their Supreme Commander  Eisenhower would oversee the operation  Allied cohesion dictated that a British  general serve as his commander in the  field during the initial stages of the  invasion  but which one Eisenhower wanted General  Alexander because the other choice  Montgomery he felt was too difficult to  work with General Brooke however  successfully convinced Churchill that  Monty was a better tactical General than  Alexander so Montgomery got the job and  was brought home to London leaving the  eighth Army behind in Italy after 18  months commanding It Montgomery worked  his magic on the assault troops making  sure to visit all of his units as he did  with the eighth Army raising morale  Monty was a proven winner and that's  what the nervous armies needed right now  after all the last successful  cross-channel Invasion had been done by  William the Conqueror 900 years before  despite many hardships on June 6 1944  the largest ambibious Landing in the  history of the world took place the  Allies successfully landed five  divisions in France 150  000 men Montgomery in addition to being  overall commander was also commander of  the 21st Army Group which consisted of  the second British army and eventually  the first Canadian Army American General  Omar Bradley meanwhile commanded the  12th Army group initially consisting of  the first Army and later joined by the  third and the ninth the Allies found it  tough going the British and Canadians  were pinned down by increasing numbers  of German troops which prevented them  from capturing the Strategic city of  Kane the Americans meanwhile found it  difficult to successfully Traverse the  countryside which was broken up into a  series of irregularly shaped and Tall  hedgerows known as barcage which the  Germans used to their advantage to make  life difficult for the Invaders  eventually Montgomery was forced to use  his British and Canadian troops like a  shield holding the Germans in place  while the Americans swung around like a  door hinge finally after two months of  battle the U.S third Army commanded by  Patton broke out and wheeled around to  the south of the Germans trapping them  in between the Americans and the British  ten thousand Germans died and 50 000  were captured in the filet pocket German  resistance crumbled at this point and  the Allies raced for Paris and the  German border a great Victory had been  won but controversy reigned over what to  do next the British believed a single  thrust by the entire group of four  armies should be made through Belgium  and Holland and then attacked the German  industrial Heartland the Aurora Valley  Eisenhower however favored a broad front  strategy which included separating the  American and British armies having the  British attacked to the North and the  Americans to the South Monty who was  also chafed Eisenhower and the latter  decided to take command of the armies  currently in the field on September the  1st leaving Montgomery only in command  of the 21st Army group once again  Montgomery can play to London claiming  Eisenhower didn't know what was going on  and questioning his Fitness to command  he also made himself look petulant when  he refused Eisenhower's invitation to  participate in the celebration  surrounding the liberation of Paris but  it was Montgomery who wasn't able to  appreciate the whole picture not  Eisenhower by this point 75 of the  troops in Europe were American with more  coming over every week the American  public would not accept a British  commander in charge of a large number of  American troops for the remainder of the  war Bradley also wanted nothing more to  do with Montgomery who he regarded as  over-cautious pompous and entirely to  preoccupied with getting its picture in  the newspaper but to the British he was  still their man hoping to placate him  somewhat Churchill promoted Monty to the  rank of field Marshal  [Music]  by September 1944 the Allied Advance was  running out of steam they were running  into the heavily fortified secret line  on the German border and their supplies  were still coming ashore in Normandy  hundreds of miles to the rear what they  needed was a deep water port on the  channel Coast like Antwerp which had  been liberated on the fourth but the  port couldn't be used because the  Germans still held the entrance to the  Shelt Estuary which controlled access to  the port many top Allied commanders  believe that Montgomery should focus on  clearing the shout above everything else  but Monty still focused on his own plan  for invading the Rural Valley in Germany  proclaimed that he had a better idea  operator Market Garden Market Garden  would see three divisions of  paratroopers dropped near the Dutch  cities of Eindhoven nikum again and  arnim to seize the river Crossings in  each one then British armored units  would race up the road to secure these  bridges that last of which in ahem was  across the Rhine River the roadway in  Germany itself if successful the Allies  could have flanked the secret line and  possibly win the war in Europe before  the end of the year the fact that the  plan was over ambitious only revealed  itself after the battle was over the  British tanks never made it on him and  the paratroopers there took Dreadful  casualties though dressed up as a  victory for propaganda purposes Market  Garden was in reality a strategic  failure that did nothing to hasten the  end of the war and cost the Allies 15  000 casualties Montgomery Chatham for  one of the first times in his life would  admit later that market Goddard was a  mistake though he took great pains to  qualify by proclaiming that if he'd been  properly supported with supplies from  headquarters that it could have been  successful belatedly Montgomery turned  his attention to Antwerp by which time  the shot had been reinforced by the bear  marked the Canadian Army would suffer  another 12 000 casualties before they  were able to open the port on November  28th in December it was the turn of the  Americans to be humiliated Hitler  failing to grasp the reality that the  war was lost gathered all of his  remaining strength through a bold strike  against the Western allies learning of  the friction between Montgomery and  Bradley he hoped to divide the two Army  groups recapture Antwerp and cut the  Allies off from their supplies if he  could force the Americans and the  British to sign A Separate Peace deal  with Germany he could then turn all of  his forces on the Soviets bearing down  on him from the east on December 16th  the Germans took the American first Army  by complete surprise suddenly attacking  from the ardenne just as they had done  to the French in 1940. four hundred  thousand men and 1 400 tanks smashed  into the American positions which  quickly gave way under the onslaught  General Bradley had been focused on the  offensives he was planning was as  flabbergasted a radio communication was  cut off so he wasn't sure what was going  on the Germans pushed West creating a  Salient or bulge in the Allied lines  newspapers dubbed the engagement the  Battle of the Bulge with things reaching  a crisis Point Eisenhower directed that  the first and Ninth American armies  which were now pinned north of the Bulge  should be temporarily transferred to  Montgomery's command Monty couldn't seem  to help but gloat at the predicament of  the Americans portraying himself as the  man who rode in and saved the day to  both his British superiors and to the  Press for a man that hardly ever  admitted to making a mistake he sure was  quick to point out perceived failures  that he saw in others for Eisenhower  this was the last straw well yes Bradley  had made a mistake there was no need to  humiliate the man that many viewed as  one of the US Army's most professional  soldiers he planned to send a message to  both Washington and London proclaiming  that either montigos or he would  realizing that he had gone too far that  Churchill would not protect him if the  cost was the risk of the entire Alliance  falling apart Montgomery was quick to  apologize Eisenhower was persuaded to  keep him in command meanwhile the German  offensive in Belgium was never destined  to do more than give the Allies a bloody  nose they didn't have enough left of  anything food fuel ammunition men to  sustain the offensive for more than two  weeks and once the Dismal winter weather  cleared and the Allies could start to  utilize their overwhelming air  superiority to attack the Germans the  offensive stalled out Fierce American  Defenders had held the line and it would  be General pattern that would get the  glory from this fight with his famous  counter-attack with the third Army  Montgomery meanwhile seemed to be  received nothing but Scorn  the Bulge had consumed the best of  Germany's remaining military strength in  the west and once it failed the entire  battle line began to collapse in March  1945 the Allies crossed the Rhine and  began racing across Germany by the start  of April the war was all but one  Montgomery believed it was possible for  him to make a dash with his army group  to Berlin to get there before the  Russians but Bradley took the wind out  of his sails when he insisted to  Eisenhower that his ninth Army which had  been under Monty's command since the  Bulge be returned to him using only his  British and Canadian troops Auntie  wouldn't be able to get to Berlin and  then he was specifically ordered not to  by Eisenhower who decided that the Elbow  River o was to be the boundary between  Western and Soviet zones Eisenhower knew  that the altar conference had already  determined that Germany and Berlin were  to be split up into occupation zones  post-war and since Berlin was within the  Soviet area why should he expend  American lives trying to capture it of  course Eisner I probably didn't realize  what Churchill was starting to do by  this point that Stalin every bit the  brutal dictator that Hitler was had no  intention of relinquishing control of  the territories that he conquered from  the Germans promises told elections  proved Hollows the Soviets set up a  series of communist satellite States in  Eastern Europe setting the stage for the  Cold War but at the time it seemed most  important to win the war against the  Nazis and the Japanese as they say  hindsight's 2020. Monty's war ended on  May the 4th when he accepted the  surrender of 